        body:
            "A story is not just a sequence of events — 'he woke up, he ate breakfast, he went to work' is a sequence, not a story. A story is a character who wants something, meets an obstacle that prevents them from getting it easily, and is changed by the struggle to overcome — or fail to overcome — that obstacle. Remove any one of those three ingredients — want, obstacle, change — and what's left is a report, not a story.\n\nThis definition matters because it explains why some technically well-written pieces still fail to hold a reader's attention. A beautifully described morning routine with no want and no obstacle is just description. A character who wants something and gets it immediately, with no real resistance, produces no tension and therefore no story. The friction between desire and obstacle is where story actually lives.\n\nThroughout this course, you will learn to build that friction deliberately — not by accident, not by 'just writing and seeing what happens,' but by understanding what each structural piece of a story is doing and why it's there. By the end, you'll be able to look at any story, yours or someone else's, and diagnose exactly which ingredient is weak when it isn't working.",
