      AppLesson(
        title: 'Character Want vs Character Need',
        body:
            "Every well-built protagonist has two layers of desire that often conflict with each other. The 'want' is the conscious, stated goal — get the promotion, win the competition, find the missing person. The 'need' is the deeper, often unconscious thing the character must confront to actually grow — learn to trust others, forgive themselves, let go of control. The want drives the plot; the need drives the theme.\n\nThe most satisfying stories are ones where getting the want requires confronting the need, whether the character likes it or not. A character who wants to win a music competition (want) might need to stop performing for her father's approval and start performing for herself (need) — and the plot forces her toward that need precisely because the competition keeps punishing her for playing it safe.\n\nA common beginner mistake is writing a character who only has a want, with no underlying need — the character then never truly changes, and readers sense something is missing even if they can't name it. Before drafting any story, write down your protagonist's want in one sentence, and their need in a separate sentence, and check that pursuing the want will eventually force a confrontation with the need.",
