        body:
            "Conflict is not limited to arguments or fights — it is any force that stands between a character and what they want in a given moment. Conflict comes in several forms: person-vs-person (two characters with incompatible goals), person-vs-self (internal doubt, fear, or competing desires), person-vs-society (norms, laws, expectations), and person-vs-nature or circumstance (a storm, an illness, bad luck).\n\nMost strong stories layer multiple types of conflict simultaneously rather than relying on just one. A character escaping a flood (person-vs-nature) might also be racing against a rival for the last boat (person-vs-person) while wrestling with guilt over leaving someone behind (person-vs-self). This layering is what makes a scene feel rich rather than thin, because the reader senses several pressures acting on the character at once, just as real difficult moments in life rarely have only one dimension.\n\nEvery scene, not just the overall plot, needs its own small conflict — a scene where a character simply gets what they came for with no resistance at all is a scene with no reason to exist. Before writing any scene, ask: what does my character want in THIS specific scene, and what or who is standing in the way of it? If you can't answer that, the scene needs a conflict injected into it or needs to be cut entirely.",
