        body:
            "Stakes are the answer to the question 'what happens if the character fails?' Without clear, meaningful stakes, even a well-built conflict feels weightless, because the reader has no reason to feel tension about the outcome. Stakes can be external (lose a job, lose a life, lose a home) or internal (lose self-respect, lose a relationship, confirm a painful belief about oneself) — the strongest scenes often carry both simultaneously.\n\nStakes need to escalate as a story progresses, or tension flattens out. If the risk in chapter one is 'she might be late for the interview' and the risk in the climax is also just 'she might be late for something,' the story has failed to build pressure. Raising stakes doesn't always mean bigger explosions — it can mean the same core risk becoming more personally costly as the character invests more of themselves in the outcome.\n\nA useful test for any draft: can you state, in one sentence, exactly what your protagonist stands to lose in the climax, and can you trace a rising line of cost from the opening to that climax? If the answer is unclear even to you as the writer, it will be far more unclear — and far less gripping — to the reader.",
