            "The three-act structure is the most widely used skeleton for stories across novels, film, and short fiction, because it mirrors a natural rhythm of setup, escalation, and resolution that audiences respond to almost universally. Act One (roughly the first quarter) establishes the character's normal world, introduces the want, and ends with an 'inciting incident' that disrupts that normal world and launches the real story.\n\nAct Two (roughly the middle half) is where the character pursues their want, faces escalating obstacles, and typically experiences a midpoint event that raises stakes further or reveals new information that changes their approach. Act Two traditionally ends with a low point — often called the 'dark night of the soul' — where the character's plan has failed and things look worse than ever.\n\nAct Three (the final quarter) is the climax and resolution — the character, having been forced to change by everything in Act Two, makes a final attempt using what they've learned, and the story resolves the central conflict, for better or worse. This isn't a rigid formula to follow mechanically, but understanding it gives you a reliable map to check your own draft against when something feels structurally 'off' but you can't identify why.",
