        body:
            "Point of view (POV) determines what information the reader has access to and how intimately they experience a character's inner world, and choosing the wrong POV for a story can quietly undermine it even when every other element is strong. First person ('I walked into the room') creates maximum intimacy with one character's voice and thoughts, but limits the reader strictly to what that character knows, sees, and understands.\n\nThird person limited ('She walked into the room') stays close to one character's perspective per scene but with slightly more narrative distance than first person, and is currently the most common choice in commercial fiction because it balances intimacy with flexibility. Third person omniscient ('She walked into the room, unaware that Tunde was watching from the hallway, already planning his next move') lets the narrator move freely between characters' minds, which offers scope but risks feeling distant if overused without discipline.\n\nWhichever POV you choose, consistency within a scene matters enormously — head-hopping (suddenly revealing a second character's private thoughts within a scene that has otherwise been tightly limited to the protagonist's perspective) is one of the most common technical errors that pulls readers out of a story, because it breaks the implicit contract about whose mind we have access to.",
