        body:
            "\"Show, don't tell\" is probably the most repeated piece of writing advice, and also one of the most frequently misunderstood. It means: instead of stating an emotion or fact directly ('Ngozi was furious'), render it through action, dialogue, sensory detail, or physical reaction ('Ngozi's hand tightened around the cup until her knuckles went pale, and when she finally spoke, her voice came out flat and controlled — too controlled'). Showing lets the reader do the work of interpreting emotion, which creates far stronger engagement than being told what to feel.\n\nHowever, 'telling' is not always wrong — it's a tool for compressing time and unimportant detail efficiently. If a character needs to travel from Lagos to Kano and nothing important happens during the trip, telling ('Three days later, she arrived in Kano') is correct; showing every mile of that uneventful journey in scene would be a pacing mistake, not good craft. The real skill is knowing which moments deserve the slowed-down, sensory treatment of showing, and which moments should be compressed with efficient telling.\n\nA practical rule: show the moments that matter emotionally or dramatically — the confrontation, the confession, the decision. Tell the connective tissue between those moments. A story that tries to 'show' absolutely everything becomes bloated and exhausting; a story that 'tells' everything important becomes flat and emotionally distant.",
