        title: 'Setting as an Active Ingredient, Not Wallpaper',
        body:
            "Weak settings function as wallpaper — a vague backdrop that could be swapped for any other location without changing the story. Strong settings function as an active ingredient — they shape what characters can and can't do, create specific texture and mood, and sometimes even generate conflict directly, like a Lagos traffic jam trapping a character who's racing to catch a flight.\n\nGrounding a setting in specific, sensory, and culturally accurate detail does more work than lengthy general description. Rather than 'the market was busy and loud,' something like 'the smell of frying akara mixed with exhaust fumes, and a trader's voice cut through the noise, quoting a price that hadn't moved in three years' gives the reader something concrete, textured, and specific to actually picture, while also revealing character and economic reality in the same breath.\n\nSetting should also reflect and reinforce a story's emotional tone. A breakup scene set in a chaotic, overcrowded danfo bus creates a very different feeling than the same conversation set in a quiet, empty church at dusk — neither is 'more correct,' but each setting choice actively shapes how the reader experiences that same emotional beat, so choose deliberately rather than defaulting to whatever location is simply convenient.",
