        body:
            "Many beginner characters are built from the outside in — a name, an age, a job, a physical description — while their actual inner life remains vague. Strong character work reverses this: start with the psychology (what do they fear most, what do they believe about themselves, what wound from their past still quietly shapes their decisions today?) and let the external details follow logically from that inner core.\n\nA character's core wound — a specific past event or ongoing circumstance that shaped how they see the world — doesn't need to be dramatic or tragic to be effective; it needs to be specific and consistently felt in how the character behaves. A man who was constantly told as a child that he talked too much might now, as an adult, stay quiet in meetings even when he has the best idea in the room — the wound explains the behavior, and the behavior, shown consistently, implies the wound without ever needing to state it directly.\n\nOnce you have the psychological core, physical details, mannerisms, speech patterns, and even a name can be chosen to reinforce and reveal that inner life rather than existing as arbitrary decoration. A character who fears losing control might have precise, controlled speech and rigid posture; these external choices become meaningful rather than random once they're anchored to something true about who the character actually is underneath.",
