      AppLesson(
        title: 'Character Arcs: Positive, Negative, and Flat',
        body:
            "A character arc is the change (or deliberate lack of change) a character undergoes across a story. A positive arc is the most common — the character grows, overcomes a flaw, or resolves their core wound in a healthier direction by the end. A negative arc is the reverse — the character is corrupted, gives in to their flaw, or ends up worse off, common in tragedies and cautionary stories.\n\nA flat arc is less commonly discussed but equally valid: the character themselves doesn't fundamentally change, but they act as a stable force of change on the world and other characters around them — many mentor figures and some genre heroes follow flat arcs, where their unwavering belief or code is precisely what allows them to transform the story's world or the people in it, rather than being transformed themselves.\n\nBefore writing, decide deliberately which arc type fits your story's theme, and make sure your plot actually supports it. A positive arc needs the plot to consistently test and eventually reward the character's growth; a negative arc needs the plot to consistently tempt or punish them toward their worse tendencies. An accidental mismatch — a plot built for growth paired with a character who never actually changes — is a common and fixable structural issue in early drafts.",
