        body:
            "Story is the full chronological sequence of everything that happens, including events that occur before your narrative even begins. Plot is the specific selection and arrangement of events you choose to actually show the reader, in whatever order best creates tension, mystery, or emotional impact — plot is story, curated and structured for maximum effect.\n\nThis distinction matters because it frees you from feeling obligated to show everything in strict chronological order. A story might contain a character's entire childhood, but the plot might open on a single afternoon twenty years later and only reveal the relevant childhood fragments through carefully placed flashback or dialogue, exactly when they'll land with the most impact.\n\nWhen a draft feels bloated or slow, it's often because story and plot have been treated as the same thing — every event that 'happened' in the story world got included in the plot, rather than the writer making deliberate choices about which events actually earn a place on the page and in what order. Plotting is an act of selection and arrangement, not simply transcription of everything that occurred.",
