      AppLesson(
        title: 'The Inciting Incident',
        body:
            "The inciting incident is the specific event that disrupts a character's normal world and sets the central story into motion — before it, the character has a want but no urgent reason to pursue it actively; after it, pursuing that want (or dealing with its consequences) becomes unavoidable. A story that delays its inciting incident too long risks losing readers during an overlong setup phase with no forward momentum yet.\n\nA strong inciting incident is specific and concrete, not vague — 'she received a letter informing her the family house was being seized in thirty days' is a clear inciting incident; 'she started feeling like something needed to change in her life' is too vague and internal to function as one, because it gives the plot nothing concrete to react to. The incident should force a decision or reaction, not just a feeling.\n\nMost commercial fiction places the inciting incident within the first 10-15% of the story's total length, though literary fiction sometimes stretches this further for deliberate atmospheric effect. Whatever length you're working with, be able to point to the exact moment your inciting incident occurs and explain specifically what changed for your character the instant after it, compared to the instant before.",
