        body:
            "Rising action is the sustained middle section where obstacles between the character and their goal grow progressively more difficult, forcing increasingly costly decisions. A common structural failure — often called 'the sagging middle' — happens when obstacles repeat at roughly the same difficulty level instead of escalating, so the middle feels like it's spinning in place rather than building toward anything.\n\nEscalation doesn't have to mean literal size or danger increasing constantly — it can mean the personal cost of pursuing the goal increasing, or the character's options narrowing, or new information recontextualizing what's actually at stake. A character willing to risk money for their goal in an early obstacle might, by a later obstacle, be forced to risk a relationship instead — the danger level in a literal sense might be similar, but the personal cost has escalated meaningfully.\n\nA practical technique for planning rising action: list every major obstacle your character will face, then rank them by cost to the character, not by external drama alone. If your ranked list doesn't already sit in ascending order, rearrange the obstacles (or intensify weaker ones) until it does — a mid-story obstacle that's harder than your climax undercuts the climax's impact entirely, regardless of how the events read individually.",
