      AppLesson(
        title: 'Foreshadowing and Planting Payoffs',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Foreshadowing is planting small, often subtle details early in a story that will pay off later, making later events feel earned and inevitable rather than random or contrived. Effective foreshadowing walks a careful line: too obvious, and the twist or reveal becomes predictable; too subtle, and readers feel the eventual payoff came from nowhere, with no fair chance to have anticipated it.\n\nA well-known technique, often called 'Chekhov's Gun,' states that if you draw specific attention to an object, detail, or fact early in a story, it should matter later — and conversely, if something matters a great deal later, it should have been planted, even subtly, earlier. A character who mentions in passing, early on, that she's terrified of open water shouldn't then casually cross a flooded river with no tension in a later chapter — either the fear needs to matter when the moment arrives, or it shouldn't have been planted as a specific detail in the first place.\n\nOn a revision pass specifically dedicated to foreshadowing, work backward from your climax and major twists: what does the reader need to have seen, however briefly, earlier in the story for this later moment to feel fair and earned rather than arbitrary? Then go back and plant that detail, naturally embedded in an earlier scene, rather than leaving the payoff to feel like a coincidence invented purely to serve the ending.",
