        title: 'The Mechanics of Dialogue Punctuation',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Before dialogue can be artful, it needs to be technically correct, because punctuation errors pull a reader's attention out of the story and onto the page itself. In standard English convention, spoken words go inside double quotation marks, with the punctuation that belongs to the dialogue itself (comma, question mark, exclamation point, or period if the sentence ends there) placed inside the closing quote mark.\n\nWhen a dialogue tag ('she said,' 'he asked') follows the spoken line, use a comma instead of a period before the closing quotation mark, unless the dialogue itself ends in a question mark or exclamation point, which can stand in for that comma: \"I'm not going,\" she said. \"Are you coming?\" he asked. Each time a different character speaks, start a new paragraph — this is a firm convention, not a style preference, and violating it makes it genuinely hard for a reader to track who is speaking.\n\nAction beats (a physical action happening alongside or instead of a dialogue tag) are typically separate sentences, not connected with a comma: \"I'm not going.\" She crossed her arms. — not \"I'm not going,\" she crossed her arms, which is a common punctuation error because crossing arms isn't a way of speaking, and can't correctly take the place of a dialogue tag like 'said' or 'asked.'",
