      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Dialogue Tags: Said Is Usually Enough',
        body:
            "'Said' and 'asked' are nearly invisible to readers — they've become so familiar that the eye slides past them without registering them as a word choice at all, which is exactly why they're the safest default tag in almost every situation. Beginner writers often reach for more 'colorful' tags — 'she exclaimed,' 'he interjected,' 'they proclaimed' — believing this adds variety, but overuse of unusual tags actually draws unwanted attention to the mechanics of the dialogue rather than to what's being said.\n\nAdverbs attached to dialogue tags ('she said angrily,' 'he asked nervously') are usually a sign that the dialogue itself, or an accompanying action beat, isn't doing enough work to convey the emotion on its own. If a line of dialogue genuinely sounds angry through its word choice and rhythm, tagging it 'she said angrily' becomes redundant; if it doesn't sound angry without the adverb doing the work, the fix is usually to rewrite the dialogue itself to carry that emotion, not to prop it up with an adverb.\n\nA useful revision exercise: go through a full scene of dialogue and delete every adverb attached to a dialogue tag, then read the scene again. If the emotional tone is still clear from the dialogue and any action beats alone, the adverbs weren't necessary. If specific lines now feel flat without them, that's useful diagnostic information about exactly which lines need to be rewritten to carry more weight on their own.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Action Beats: An Alternative to Tags',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "An action beat is a small physical action placed near a line of dialogue instead of, or alongside, a dialogue tag, and it does double duty: it clarifies who's speaking (functioning like a tag) while also adding visual, physical texture to the scene that a tag alone can't provide. \"I don't believe you.\" She set down her cup, carefully, as if it might shatter — the action beat here does more emotional work than 'she said skeptically' ever could, because it shows controlled tension through a specific physical choice rather than naming the emotion directly.\n\nAction beats are especially useful for breaking up long stretches of back-and-forth dialogue that would otherwise read as a flat list of quoted lines with no sense of physical space or movement — a scene entirely composed of dialogue with no beats at all can start to feel like a script rather than prose, floating in a physical vacuum with no bodies, no room, no gestures grounding it.\n\nBe careful that action beats stay purposeful rather than becoming nervous filler — a beat should reveal character, emotion, or subtext, not just exist to break up dialogue for its own sake ('he shrugged,' 'she nodded,' repeated identically every few lines with no real specificity or variation quickly becomes as invisible and unhelpful as an overused dialogue tag, just in physical form instead of verbal.",
