      AppLesson(
        title: 'Subtext: What Characters Don\'t Say',
        body:
            "Subtext is the meaning underneath the literal words of a line of dialogue — what a character actually feels or wants, which they express indirectly rather than stating plainly, either because they're unable to articulate it, unwilling to admit it, or actively trying to hide it from another character. Dialogue rich with subtext feels more true to real conversation, because real people very rarely say exactly and only what they mean, especially about difficult emotional topics.\n\nA scene where two former friends discuss, on the surface, whose turn it is to pay for dinner can carry enormous subtext about a deeper unresolved resentment between them, if written with that subtext deliberately in mind — every line about the bill becomes a proxy battle for something neither character is willing to name directly. This kind of scene is often more powerful than one where the characters simply state their resentment aloud, because the tension of watching them circle the real issue creates active reader engagement.\n\nTo build subtext deliberately, identify what a character actually wants to say in a scene, then write dialogue where they say almost anything except that — deflecting, changing the subject, answering a different question than the one asked, or focusing intensely on some smaller, safer detail instead. The gap between what's said and what's meant is where subtext lives, and readers are often more moved by sensing that gap than they would be by having it explained to them directly.",
        codeSnippet:
