      AppLesson(
        title: 'Dialect, Accent, and Writing Regional Speech',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Representing regional speech, dialect, or a character's accent on the page is a genuinely tricky balance — done well, it adds authenticity and specificity; done poorly, through heavy phonetic misspelling ('Ah'm gwan tell yuh'), it can become distracting to read and, particularly when applied to marginalized dialects, can feel disrespectful or reduce a character to caricature rather than a full person.\n\nMost professional fiction handles regional or dialectal speech primarily through word choice, rhythm, and grammar patterns rather than heavy phonetic respelling — a Nigerian Pidgin-speaking character's voice can be conveyed through characteristic sentence structure and specific vocabulary ('I don tell am already,' 'no wahala') without needing every single word forced into non-standard spelling, which keeps the dialogue both authentic-sounding and comfortably readable.\n\nA light touch, applied consistently, usually beats a heavy touch applied inconsistently. Choose a handful of genuinely characteristic speech patterns for a character with a distinct dialect or accent, apply them consistently throughout the story, and trust the reader's imagination to fill in the actual sound once the pattern is established — over-marking every single line with dialect spelling tends to slow reading and, past a certain density, can start to feel like the writer is drawing attention to the character's otherness rather than simply letting them speak naturally.",
