      AppLesson(
        title: 'Pacing Dialogue: Rhythm and White Space',
        body:
            "Dialogue-heavy scenes tend to read faster than dense narrative prose, partly because of the visual white space created by frequent paragraph breaks and short lines — this makes dialogue a useful pacing tool for speeding up a scene that's felt slow, or for creating a sense of rapid, tense back-and-forth during a high-stakes confrontation.\n\nSentence length within dialogue also controls pacing and emotional tone. Short, clipped exchanges ('Where is he?' 'Gone.' 'Since when?' 'This morning.') create urgency and tension through their sheer brevity and rhythm, while longer, more meandering dialogue can convey a character who's stalling, rambling from nervousness, or simply comfortable and unhurried in that particular moment.\n\nVary sentence length deliberately across a scene rather than settling into one uniform rhythm throughout — a scene made entirely of short, clipped lines can become exhausting or feel monotone after enough exchanges, just as a scene of only long, flowing lines can start to feel slow regardless of the actual content. Mixing rhythm, matched intentionally to the emotional beats of the scene, keeps dialogue feeling alive rather than mechanically uniform.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Group Dialogue: Managing More Than Two Speakers',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Dialogue between two characters is relatively straightforward to track for a reader, since attribution can often be inferred from context alone once established. Dialogue involving three or more speakers is considerably harder to manage clearly, because the reader can lose track of exactly who's speaking if tags and action beats aren't handled with extra care and deliberate attention.\n\nIn group scenes, tag or attribute speech more frequently than you would in a two-person scene, and vary how you do it — sometimes a direct tag ('Amara said'), sometimes an action beat that clearly identifies the speaker through a specific physical action or position in the room, and occasionally letting a very distinctive voice speak without any tag at all, once that character's voice is established enough to be recognizable on its own.\n\nA practical technique for complex group scenes is briefly grounding the reader in each character's physical position in the space before the dialogue begins in earnest — knowing that one character is by the door, another at the table, and a third pacing near the window gives the reader a mental map that makes it easier to track shifting attention and speaker changes throughout the rest of the scene, without needing constant explicit tagging.",
