      AppLesson(
        title: 'Internal Monologue vs Spoken Dialogue',
        body:
            "Internal monologue — a character's unspoken thoughts — is a distinct tool from spoken dialogue, and conflating the two, or handling the transition between them sloppily, is a common source of reader confusion. In most contemporary prose conventions, internal thought is rendered either in italics for a direct, unfiltered thought ('This is a mistake, she thought'), or woven into the narration itself without quotation marks or italics, in close third-person or first-person POV.\n\nThe contrast between what a character says aloud and what they're actually thinking is a powerful source of both subtext and dramatic irony, letting the reader access a private truth that other characters in the scene don't have. A character who calmly says 'take your time' aloud while internally thinking 'we are going to miss this flight because of you' creates immediate tension and characterization through that direct contrast between spoken and internal text.\n\nUse internal monologue with some restraint, particularly in scenes that are otherwise dialogue-heavy — constantly cutting into a character's private thoughts between every line of spoken dialogue can slow a scene's momentum and undercut the tension that comes from readers having to infer meaning from dialogue and subtext alone, the same skill built in the earlier lesson on subtext specifically.",
