      AppLesson(
        title: 'Editing Dialogue: Cutting the Fat',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "First-draft dialogue is almost always longer and more literal than it needs to be, because writers often draft dialogue the way real people actually talk — full of greetings, small talk, restated information, and social pleasantries that serve real conversation but slow fictional dialogue down without adding story value. Editing dialogue is largely an act of cutting: removing the parts real speech includes that fiction doesn't need.\n\nA useful editing technique is cutting the first and last lines of a dialogue exchange, specifically — beginners often draft a scene starting with a greeting ('Hey, how are you?' 'I'm fine, and you?') and ending with a farewell, when the actual substance of the scene begins and ends somewhere in the middle. Trimming these social bookends and starting closer to the real point of tension almost always improves pacing immediately.\n\nAlso watch for characters restating information the reader already knows, purely for the other character's benefit — if the reader already learned a fact in an earlier scene, a character explaining that same fact again to another character, at length, often needs to be compressed or cut entirely, unless the retelling itself reveals something new about the character doing the retelling.",
