        body:
            "For your final project, write a complete scene (800-1,200 words) between two characters, told almost entirely through dialogue and minimal action beats, with no direct narration of internal thought or exposition. The scene must reveal a significant piece of information or emotional shift purely through what the characters say — and, more importantly, through what they don't say directly.\n\nBefore writing, complete the subtext-planning template from earlier in this course for both characters, identifying what each actually wants to say versus what they'll say instead. Ensure your dialogue punctuation and paragraph breaks follow correct convention throughout, and use action beats purposefully rather than as nervous filler between lines.\n\nWhen finished, read the scene aloud, ideally to someone else, and ask them what they believe the characters were really feeling or hiding, based only on the dialogue as written. If their answer matches what you intended, your subtext is landing. If they take the dialogue at face value with none of the intended undertone, the scene needs another pass to strengthen the gap between what's said and what's meant.",
