      AppLesson(
        title: 'What Makes Short Fiction Different from a Novel',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "A short story is not simply a smaller novel — it's a fundamentally different form with its own discipline. Where a novel can afford multiple subplots, a large ensemble cast, and a slowly unfolding arc across hundreds of pages, a short story typically has room for one central conflict, one primary character arc (sometimes two), and a much narrower window of time or event.\n\nBecause of this compression, short stories usually work best when they focus on a single, pivotal moment or turning point in a character's life, rather than trying to cover a sprawling span of time. A story might cover a single afternoon, a single conversation, or a single decision — and derive its power from how deeply and specifically it explores that narrow window, rather than from breadth of coverage.\n\nRevision in short fiction is disproportionately about cutting rather than adding, precisely because the form has so little room for anything that isn't essential. Every scene, every line of dialogue, every descriptive detail needs to justify its place far more rigorously than in a novel, where there's simply more space to absorb material that isn't pulling its full weight.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Starting In Medias Res: Skip the Slow Wind-Up',
        body:
            "'In medias res' means starting a story already in the middle of the action, rather than opening with extended setup, backstory, or a character's ordinary routine before anything interesting happens. Short fiction, with its limited space, generally cannot afford a slow wind-up the way a novel occasionally can — readers need a reason to keep reading within the first few sentences, not the first few pages.\n\nA strong short story opening typically drops the reader into a moment already charged with tension, question, or unusual detail — not necessarily an action scene, but something that immediately signals 'something here is not settled or ordinary,' inviting the reader to keep reading in order to understand what's happening and why. Compare a slow opening ('It was a normal Tuesday morning when Ade woke up and got ready for work as usual') against an in medias res opening ('Ade had already decided he wasn't going to tell his wife about the letter, not yet, maybe not ever') — the second immediately raises a question the reader wants answered.\n\nA useful revision technique specifically for short stories: identify the actual first moment of genuine tension or interest in your draft, and consider cutting everything before it, even if that means losing pages of setup you originally wrote. Much of that earlier material, if genuinely necessary, can often be compressed into a single line or worked in through implication once the story is already moving.",
