      AppLesson(
        title: 'Compression: Doing More With Less',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Compression is the central technical skill of short fiction — conveying character, setting, and emotional weight in far fewer words than a novel would use for the same information, without the result feeling rushed or thin. This is achieved primarily through careful, specific detail selection rather than comprehensive coverage: one precisely chosen detail can imply an entire life or relationship, where ten generic details would take far longer to say far less.\n\nA character's entire troubled marriage can be implied through a single small, specific action — the way she automatically sets two plates at dinner out of habit, months after he moved out — rather than through paragraphs of explicit backstory about the relationship's decline. This kind of compressed, implication-heavy detail respects the reader's intelligence and trusts them to infer meaning, which is both more efficient and often more emotionally resonant than direct explanation.\n\nPractice compression directly: take a paragraph of exposition you've written explaining a character's situation, and challenge yourself to convey the same essential information and emotional weight in a single sentence of concrete action or image instead. This exercise, repeated across a full draft, is often what separates a baggy, overlong short story from a tight, resonant one at the same approximate length.",
        codeSnippet:
