      AppLesson(
        title: 'The Single Effect: Poe\'s Principle',
        body:
            "Edgar Allan Poe argued that a short story should be built entirely toward a single, unified emotional effect, and that every element — every scene, every sentence — should serve that one effect rather than pulling the reader in multiple directions. This principle remains genuinely useful for modern short fiction: before drafting, it's worth being able to state in one sentence what single emotional effect you want the reader left with by the final line.\n\nThis doesn't mean a short story can only contain one emotion throughout its length — it can and often should move through several before arriving at its intended final effect — but it does mean every scene should be building toward that eventual landing point, rather than existing as an interesting but disconnected detour that doesn't actually serve where the story is ultimately headed.\n\nIf you find a scene in your draft that's genuinely well-written and interesting on its own terms but doesn't clearly contribute to the story's single intended effect, it's a strong candidate for cutting, however much you might like it as an individual piece of writing — this is one of the hardest and most valuable disciplines of short story revision, distinct from novel revision where a well-written tangent has more room to justify its own existence.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Endings That Land: Resonance Over Resolution',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Short story endings often work differently from novel endings — rather than fully resolving every plot thread, many of the most memorable short stories end on a moment of resonance, realization, or a final, carefully chosen image that reframes everything the reader has just experienced, without necessarily explaining exactly what happens next. This isn't a cop-out; done well, it's often more powerful than a fully explained resolution, because it trusts the reader to complete the emotional arc themselves.\n\nA strong short story ending typically connects back to something established earlier — an image, an object, a piece of dialogue — now returned to with new meaning because of everything that's happened in between. A story that opens on a character carefully setting an alarm clock might close on that same alarm clock, now meaningless because the reason they needed to wake up early no longer exists — the image is the same, but its weight has completely changed, and the reader feels that shift without needing it explained.\n\nAvoid the common beginner instinct to over-explain the ending, spelling out exactly what the character learned or exactly how they feel in the story's final lines — trust the concrete, specific final image or moment to carry the emotional weight, and resist the urge to add a summarizing sentence afterward that tells the reader what to feel about what they just read. If the ending needs that explanation to work, the ending itself likely needs to be sharpened, not supplemented.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Word Count Discipline and Where to Submit',
        body:
            "Short fiction has fairly standard length categories that matter practically, especially if you intend to submit to literary magazines or competitions: flash fiction is typically under 1,000 words (sometimes as short as 100), short-short stories run roughly 1,000-2,500 words, and traditional short stories generally run 2,500-7,500 words, with anything longer often classified as a novelette or novella instead.\n\nMost literary magazines and competitions specify exact word count limits, and submissions that exceed them are often rejected without being read at all, regardless of quality — always check and respect a publication's stated word count before submitting, since this is one of the easiest and most avoidable ways to be disqualified from consideration purely on a technicality unrelated to the writing itself.\n\nIf your draft consistently runs longer than your target category, resist simply cutting scenes to hit a number — instead, revisit the compression techniques from this course and look for places where the same content and emotional weight can be conveyed in fewer words, preserving the story's actual scope while tightening its execution, which almost always produces a stronger story than mechanical scene deletion done purely to satisfy a word count.",
