      AppLesson(
        title: 'The Twist Ending: When It Works and When It Doesn\'t',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "A twist ending recontextualizes everything the reader has read, revealing new information that changes the meaning of prior events. Twists work best when they're genuinely earned by fair, honest foreshadowing planted earlier — the reader should be able to look back after the twist and see, in retrospect, that the clues were there all along, even if they didn't consciously register them on a first read.\n\nA twist that relies entirely on withheld information the reader had no fair way to access — a random, unforeshadowed fact revealed only in the final paragraph purely to shock — tends to feel like a cheat rather than a satisfying reveal, because it wasn't actually possible for an attentive reader to have anticipated or participated in discovering it. The best twists make the reader feel clever for almost seeing it coming, not tricked for having no chance at all.\n\nNot every short story needs or benefits from a twist — many of the most acclaimed short stories end with quiet resonance rather than a structural surprise, and forcing a twist onto a story that doesn't naturally call for one often produces a contrived, gimmicky effect. Before adding a twist, honestly assess whether your story's themes and setup genuinely support one, or whether you're reaching for a twist simply because it feels like short stories are 'supposed' to have one.",
