      AppLesson(
        title: 'Revising a Short Story: The Line-Level Pass',
        body:
            "Once a short story's structure is solid, a dedicated line-level revision pass is essential, precisely because the form's compression means every individual sentence carries more relative weight than in a longer work. Read through specifically hunting for unnecessary words — qualifiers like 'very,' 'somewhat,' 'a little,' redundant phrases, and any sentence that could say the same thing in fewer words without losing meaning or rhythm.\n\nCheck your opening and closing lines with special scrutiny, since short fiction lives and dies disproportionately on these two points — a strong middle can't fully rescue a weak opening that fails to hook a reader in the first place, or a weak ending that fails to land the emotional payoff the rest of the story built toward.\n\nRead the entire story aloud in one sitting, listening specifically for rhythm and pacing rather than content at this stage — awkward sentence construction, unintentional repetition of words in close proximity, and pacing that drags or rushes tend to become far more obvious to the ear than to the eye during a silent read, and a short story's compressed length makes this full read-aloud pass genuinely feasible in a single sitting, unlike with a novel.",
