            "A believable fictional culture needs internal logic — its norms, values, and taboos should feel like they emerged from that society's actual history, environment, and needs, rather than feeling arbitrarily assigned. Ask why a given custom exists: a culture that forbids eating a specific animal might have a practical historical root (that animal was once sacred to a now-vanished religion, or it was the only reliable food source during a historical famine, making its consumption taboo out of communal memory) rather than existing for no discernible reason.\n\nAvoid designing a fictional culture as a single monolithic block with no internal variation — real cultures and societies contain generational differences, regional variation, class distinctions, and dissenting subcultures who push back against dominant norms, and reflecting even a little of this internal diversity makes a fictional culture feel considerably more real than a uniform, single-note society where everyone believes and behaves identically.\n\nDraw on real-world cultural research, including your own cultural background and knowledge if relevant, to ground invented cultures in genuine, specific detail rather than generic 'fantasy medieval Europe' or equally generic tropes drawn from other fiction rather than lived reality — Nigerian and broader African history, politics, and social structures offer enormously rich, underused material for building specific, believable fictional societies that don't default to the same handful of overused Western fantasy templates.",
