        body:
            "A world's history shouldn't function as a static, disconnected timeline of events that happened once and then stopped mattering — a well-built history has living echoes that continue to shape the present story: old grudges between nations, inherited social hierarchies from a past conquest, cultural trauma passed down through generations, or infrastructure and institutions built during a now-vanished historical era that people still live within and adapt to today.\n\nRather than designing history as a complete list of dates and rulers, focus on a handful of specific past events that directly and visibly shape your story's present-day conflicts — a war two generations ago that still explains current border tensions and lingering prejudice is more useful, narratively, than a fully detailed thousand-year timeline where none of it actually connects to what's happening in your current story.\n\nCharacters' personal relationship to history — what they were taught, what they believe, what their family personally experienced — often matters more dramatically than the 'objective' historical facts of your world as the author understands them. Two characters from opposing sides of an old historical conflict may have been taught completely different, incompatible versions of the same past event, and that gap in understanding can itself become a rich, specific source of tension in the present story.",
