      AppLesson(
        title: 'Exposition: Delivering World Information Without Info-Dumping',
        body:
            "Info-dumping — delivering large blocks of worldbuilding explanation directly to the reader, disconnected from immediate story action — is one of the most common and most avoidable worldbuilding weaknesses, because it stops the story's forward momentum to deliver information the reader hasn't yet been given a reason to actually want or need.\n\nThe strongest approach is delivering world information exactly when the plot creates genuine need for it, in the smallest dose that satisfies that immediate need, ideally through action, dialogue, or specific sensory detail rather than direct authorial explanation. A character navigating a market can reveal an entire economic and social system through what they specifically notice, buy, and avoid, without a single sentence of direct exposition about 'how the economy works here.'\n\nTrust readers to piece together larger patterns from smaller, specific, well-placed details across multiple scenes rather than needing everything explained comprehensively and immediately in one place — readers, especially of genre fiction with worldbuilding as a genuine pleasure of the form, often actively enjoy the process of gradually inferring how an invented world works, and delivering everything upfront and fully explained can rob them of that specific pleasure.",
