        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Every experienced writer's first draft is, by design, worse than their final one — the common phrase 'the first draft is just you telling yourself the story' captures something real: a first draft's job is to get the story to exist at all, in some rough form, so that revision has actual material to work with. Expecting a first draft to be polished is a common source of discouragement that stops many writers from finishing anything.\n\nUnderstanding this in advance changes how you should approach drafting itself — write forward without excessive self-editing mid-draft, accepting that scenes will be clumsy, some dialogue will be flat, and structural problems will exist, because all of that is fixable in revision, but a story that never gets finished because the writer kept perfecting early chapters instead of finishing the draft is much harder to fix.\n\nRevision is not the same skill as drafting, and treating them as genuinely separate stages — finish the full draft first, then revise as a distinct second phase with a different mindset — tends to produce better finished work than trying to perfect each chapter before moving to the next, because early revision often happens before you actually know what the story as a whole needs, since later chapters frequently reveal what earlier ones should have set up.",
