      AppLesson(
        title: 'The Cooling-Off Period',
        body:
            "Immediately after finishing a draft, most writers are too close to their own work to revise it effectively — you know what you meant to convey so intimately that it's genuinely difficult to see clearly whether you actually conveyed it on the page, or whether you're simply reading your own intention into the words. A cooling-off period — deliberately setting the draft aside for a period of time, ideally at least a week or two, sometimes longer for a full novel — creates enough distance to read your own work with something closer to a stranger's eyes.\n\nDuring the cooling-off period, resist the urge to reread or tinker with the draft at all — the goal is genuine distance, and even small edits during this window can interfere with the fresh perspective you're trying to create. Working on something entirely different during this time, rather than simply waiting idly, is a common and effective practice among working writers.\n\nWhen you finally return to the draft after cooling off, read it in as few sittings as possible, ideally start to finish without stopping to fix problems as you notice them — take notes on what isn't working, but resist the urge to fix anything during this first read-through. Fixing during a first read-through pulls you back into the same close, non-objective relationship to the text you were trying to escape, and you'll likely miss larger structural patterns by getting absorbed in line-level fixes too early.",
