        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "One of the most common and costly revision mistakes is line-editing prose before confirming the story's underlying structure actually works — polishing individual sentences in a scene that needs to be cut entirely, or moved to a different position in the story, wastes real effort and creates emotional attachment to sentences that shouldn't have survived the structural pass in the first place.\n\nAlways address structural issues first: does the plot follow a coherent escalation, does the character arc actually track and pay off, are there scenes that don't serve the story's central conflict or theme and should be cut or majorly reworked, does the pacing sag anywhere significant? These big-picture questions should be resolved, at least in broad strokes, before investing serious time in sentence-level polish that might end up deleted anyway once structural problems are fixed.\n\nA practical technique for structural revision: write a one-sentence summary of every single scene in your draft, then read through just those summaries, ignoring the actual prose entirely. This distilled view makes structural problems — repetitive scenes, missing escalation, an unclear or absent midpoint, a rushed ending — much easier to spot than they are buried within full, polished prose that can disguise structural weakness through sheer sentence-level competence.",
