      AppLesson(
        title: 'Pacing Diagnostics: Finding the Slow Parts',
        body:
            "Pacing problems are often felt by readers before they can be clearly articulated — a section 'drags' without an obvious, easily nameable cause. A useful diagnostic is tracking tension level scene by scene on a simple numeric scale, which often reveals a visual pattern (a long flat stretch with no rise, for instance) that's harder to spot just from reading the prose directly, absorbed in sentence-level detail.\n\nCommon, specific causes of sagging pace include: scenes where the character's goal or the obstacle to it is unclear or weak, extended sections of description or internal reflection with no forward movement in the plot, and repeated beats where a similar conflict or realization happens more than once without genuinely escalating or adding new information.\n\nOnce a slow section is identified, the fix is rarely simply 'cut it' — often the scene contains necessary information or character development, but needs restructuring: compressing several slow beats into one sharper scene, injecting a clearer and more specific conflict into an otherwise flat scene, or moving necessary information to a different point in the story where it can be delivered more efficiently, embedded within a scene that already has stronger momentum of its own.",
