      AppLesson(
        title: 'Consistency Checks: Continuity and Logic',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Continuity errors — a character's eye color changing between chapters, a described injury disappearing without explanation, a timeline that doesn't add up when checked closely — are common in any draft of meaningful length, because it's genuinely difficult to hold every detail in memory across a long writing process that may span weeks or months.\n\nA dedicated continuity pass, separate from other revision work, is worth doing specifically: create a simple reference document tracking key physical details (character appearances, ages, specific dates or timeline markers, established facts about the world or setting) as you encounter them while rereading, and flag any place where a later detail contradicts an earlier established one.\n\nLogic checks go beyond simple continuity into plot mechanics: does a character have a plausible reason to be where they are in a given scene? Does information one character reveals actually match what they would realistically know at that point in the story? Small logic gaps like these are exactly the kind of thing attentive readers notice and lose trust over, even when the prose itself is otherwise strong and the overall story is compelling.",
