      AppLesson(
        title: 'Cutting: The Hardest and Most Valuable Skill',
        body:
            "Cutting material you've already written, sometimes material you're genuinely proud of on a sentence level, is emotionally difficult but is consistently one of the single most valuable revision skills a writer can develop — a tighter draft with only essential material almost always reads as stronger than a longer draft padded with material that's individually well-written but not actually necessary to the story's core function.\n\nA useful mental reframe: cut material isn't wasted or deleted forever in any meaningful sense — save cut scenes and passages in a separate document rather than deleting them outright, which makes the emotional act of cutting considerably easier (nothing is truly \"lost\"), and occasionally cut material genuinely does find a home in a different project or a different point in the same story later on.\n\nA specific test for any scene or passage under consideration for cutting: if you removed it entirely, would a reader be confused, or would the story simply continue working without them noticing anything was missing? If the honest answer is that nothing essential would be lost, the material — however well-crafted on a sentence level — is very likely a strong candidate for cutting, regardless of how much personal attachment you feel to it.",
