      AppLesson(
        title: 'Reading Your Work Aloud',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Reading your own prose aloud, in its entirety, surfaces problems that are genuinely difficult to catch through silent reading alone — awkward rhythm, unintentionally repeated words in close proximity, sentences that are grammatically correct but physically difficult to actually say without stumbling, and dialogue that doesn't sound like real speech once you're forced to voice it rather than simply scan it visually.\n\nThis technique works because reading aloud slows you down and engages a different part of your attention than silent reading does — silent reading lets your eye skip ahead and your brain fill in gaps automatically, smoothing over problems that become immediately, undeniably obvious the moment you have to physically produce each word in sequence, out loud, in real time.\n\nFor dialogue specifically, reading aloud (ideally with a different vocal approach for each character, even if you're not a trained performer) tests whether each character's voice actually sounds distinct, or whether every character has been unintentionally written to sound essentially like the author's own natural voice and rhythm, regardless of who's supposedly speaking in that specific scene.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Getting and Using Feedback',
        body:
            "Feedback from other readers is valuable precisely because it counters the blindness every writer has toward their own work — but not all feedback is equally useful, and learning to interpret it well is its own distinct skill separate from simply receiving it. When a reader says a specific moment 'didn't work' for them, that observation is almost always reliable and worth taking seriously; when they suggest a specific fix for it, that suggestion is far less reliable, because they're not the author and don't have your same full understanding of the story's needs.\n\nSeek feedback from multiple readers where genuinely possible, and pay closer attention to patterns that repeat across several readers than to any single reader's individual, isolated reaction — if only one reader out of five found a particular section confusing, that's useful but not necessarily urgent information; if four out of five independently flagged the same section, that's a much stronger, more reliable signal that something genuinely needs revision there.\n\nWhen receiving feedback, resist the urge to explain, defend, or justify your choices in the moment — your job during a feedback conversation is to listen and understand the reader's actual experience of the text, not to convince them they experienced it wrong. You can evaluate and decide what to actually do with any given piece of feedback later, privately, with a clearer head once the conversation itself is over.",
