        body:
            "Proofreading — the final technical pass checking spelling, grammar, punctuation, and formatting consistency — should happen last, after all structural, pacing, and line-level revision is complete, because proofreading a section that's about to be cut or majorly rewritten is wasted effort that will simply need to be redone once that section's content is finalized.\n\nProofreading your own work is notoriously difficult for the same reason self-editing generally is: your brain automatically corrects small errors while reading your own familiar text, effectively hiding them from you even on a careful pass. Reading backward from the end of a document, sentence by sentence rather than in natural reading order, is a genuinely effective technique for catching typos specifically, precisely because it disrupts your brain's automatic, meaning-driven reading pattern and forces closer attention to each individual sentence in isolation.\n\nChecking formatting consistency matters especially for anything intended for submission — consistent use of italics for internal thought or foreign words, consistent chapter heading style, consistent scene-break formatting, and correct, consistent manuscript formatting (standard font, spacing, and margins) if submitting to agents or publishers who typically have specific formatting expectations that differ from how a manuscript might look while you're actively drafting it for yourself.",
